<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jagiellonian dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jagiellonian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jagiellonian dynasty was a royal house that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and at times <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> from the late fourteenth to the late sixteenth century. It began with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a>'s marriage to Jadwiga in 1386. Its long rule bound Poland and Lithuania together and led to the formation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> in 1569.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-dynasty.jpg" alt="Jagiellonian dynasty" width="320" height="305" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wereszczyński · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:COA_Jagiellon.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The dynasty was a cadet branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediminids">Gediminids</a>, the ruling house of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>, and takes its name from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a>, whose name means strong rider in Lithuanian. The Piast rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> had ended with the death of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iii-the-great">Casimir III the Great</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-krewo">Union of Krewo</a>, Jogaila agreed to be baptised and to marry the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jadwiga-of-poland">Queen Jadwiga</a> of Poland, and as Władysław II Jagiełło he became king as her co-ruler. Jadwiga died in 1399, and as their only daughter had died as an infant, Jogaila's descendants had no inherited right to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a>. He remained king as an elected ruler, and later had four more children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Members of the dynasty were Kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> from 1386 to 1572, Grand Dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> in 1377–1392 and 1440–1572, Kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in 1440–1444 and 1490–1526, and Kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> from 1471 to 1526. The union strengthened Poland and Lithuania in their shared opposition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> and the growing power of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, and linked states on the two sides of the divide between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Byzantine worlds. Władysław III briefly ruled both Poland and Hungary from 1440 to 1444, and Vladislaus II, son of Casimir IV, ruled Bohemia and Hungary from 1490, moving his court to Hungary.</p><p>In 1515 a congress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> arranged a succession agreement and marriages between the Jagiellons and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> of Maximilian I. The Ottoman expansion damaged the last Jagiellon monarchies: Suleiman I took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> in 1521, and in 1526 the Hungarian army was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohacs">Battle of Mohács</a>, where Louis II Jagiellon was killed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> was afterwards partitioned between the Habsburgs and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> the dynasty ended with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus">Sigismund II Augustus</a>, whose heirs were his sisters; Catherine's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-iii-vasa">Sigismund III Vasa</a> joined the Polish branch to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-vasa">House of Vasa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The sixteenth century, especially the reigns of Sigismund I and Sigismund II, the last two Jagiellonian kings, is most often identified with the Polish Golden Age and the rise of Polish Renaissance culture, built on the prosperity of the landed nobility and the townspeople of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a>. The Jagiellons were polyglots, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus">Sigismund II Augustus</a> kept separate Lithuanian-speaking and Polish-speaking courts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-university">Jagiellonian University</a> and its library in Kraków preserve the name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jagiellonian dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its dynastic union with Poland, begun at Krewo in 1385, produced the <b>Jagiellonian dynasty</b>, which at times ruled Poland, Lithuania, Bohemia and Hungary, and ultimately the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Polish nobles preferred to keep the <b>Jagiellonian dynasty</b> but to choose among its sons, believing that princes who had to earn the crown would be raised to be more capable.</blockquote><a href="/government/elective-monarchy">Elective monarchy · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>Sigismund II Augustus, last of the male <b>Jagiellons</b>, had no heir.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-poland-1385-1569">Kingdom of Poland (1385-1569)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-house"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Rulers and members</h2><figure class="pp-chart"><p class="cty-scale" aria-hidden="true"><span>1375</span><span>1500</span><span>1625</span></p><ol class="cty-tl pp-tl"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o"><img class="pp-av" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="28" height="28"><span class="pp-nm"><span class="cty-n">Władysław II Jagiełło</span><span class="cty-y">r. 1386–1434</span></span></a><span class="cty-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="cty-bar pp-dim" style="left:4.4%;width:19.2%"></span></span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-iii-of-poland"><img class="pp-av" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/wladyslaw-iii-of-poland.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="28" height="28"><span class="pp-nm"><span class="cty-n">Władysław III of Poland</span><span class="cty-y">1424–1444</span></span></a><span class="cty-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="cty-bar pp-life" style="left:19.6%;width:8.0%"></span></span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon"><img class="pp-av" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/casimir-iv-jagiellon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="28" height="28"><span class="pp-nm"><span class="cty-n">Casimir IV Jagiellon</span><span class="cty-y">1427–1492</span></span></a><span class="cty-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="cty-bar pp-life" style="left:20.8%;width:26.0%"></span></span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/vladislaus-ii-of-hungary"><img class="pp-av" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/vladislaus-ii-of-hungary.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="28" height="28"><span class="pp-nm"><span class="cty-n">Vladislaus II of Hungary</span><span class="cty-y">1456–1516</span></span></a><span class="cty-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="cty-bar pp-life" style="left:32.4%;width:24.0%"></span></span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/hedwig-jagiellon-duchess-of-bavaria"><img class="pp-av" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/h